    <article xml:id="notes">

<section xml:id="notes-week-09">
       <title>Districting Algorithms</title>

      <definition>
        <p>
          A <term>graph</term> <m>G</m> is pair of sets: <m>V = \{v_1,\dots,v_n\}</m> (the <term>vertex set</term>) and <m>E \subseteq \{\{v_i,v_j\} \, \colon \, 1 \leq i,j \leq n\}</m> (the <term>edge set</term>). A <term>weighted graph</term> has a numerical value associated to each vertex.
        </p>
      </definition>

      <example>
        <p> 
          an example of a graph goes here 
        </p>
      </example>

      <p>Notice that the way we draw the graph is not part of the graph itself!</p>

      <example>
        <p> 
          an example of the same graph drawn differently goes here 
        </p>
      </example>

      <definition>
        <p>
          If <m> e = \{v,w\} \in E</m>, we say <m>e</m> is <term>incident</term> to <m>v</m> and <m>w</m> and that <m>v</m> and <m>w</m> are <term>adjacent</term>. The <term>degree</term> of a vertex is the number of edges incident to it.
        </p>
      </definition>


      <definition> 
        <p>
          A <term>cycle</term> is a... , a <term>tree</term> is a graph without cycles; a vertex of degree one in a tree is called a <term>leaf</term>, a path is a tree with only two leaves.
        </p>
      </definition>

      <p>Given a graph <m>G</m>, a <term>minimal spanning tree</term> is a tree that reaches all the vertices of <m>G</m>
      
    </section>


